Write the subject in one pass; do not generate alternatives or revise a subject that already matches the grammar. Grammar, exactly: `<kind>(<scope>)?!?: <description>`. `<kind>` is lowercase, one of feat/fix/docs/refactor/test/style/perf/build/ci/chore — use `chore` only for repo maintenance, tooling, config, or dependency changes. `<scope>` is optional and may contain only lowercase letters, digits, `/`, `_`, `-` (no dots, no uppercase); omit it and its parentheses when no single subsystem owns most of the diff. Exactly one space follows the colon and `<description>` starts with a lowercase letter or digit. Write the frontmatter value unquoted — quotes become part of the subject and are rejected. There is no length limit. Say what the change does in lowercase imperative mood; never say that files changed, and never use "update", "misc", "stuff", or file counts. Omit the body only for a one-line typo, formatting, or comment change whose subject already states the whole change; every other commit gets a body explaining WHY. Default to a single `## Body` item; use the trio `## Body Summary` / `## Body Details` / `## Body Footer` instead — never alongside `## Body` — only when there are migration steps, breaking changes, or issue references for the footer. Bad "chore: update files" -> Good "feat(mcp): add structured commit retries" with body. Bad "fix: stuff" -> Good "fix(parser): preserve prefixed transcript lines" with body.

Document shape: a markdown document whose frontmatter carries the decision. For a commit, use `type: commit` plus `subject:` in the frontmatter, then either one `## Body` section OR the detailed trio (one `- [ID] text` item per section, never both), plus optional `## Files` (one item per file) and `## Excluded Files` (items formatted `path | reason`; valid reasons: internal_ignore, not_task_related, sensitive, deferred). When no commit is needed, submit only frontmatter `type: skip` with `reason: <why>`.

